# C# Style

## Braces
Always use `{}` for control flow. Never put the body on the same line as the condition.

```csharp
// BAD
if (condition) return;
if (condition)
    return;

// GOOD
if (condition)
{
    return;
}
```

Applies to `if`, `else`, `for`, `foreach`, `while`, `do-while`, `switch`, and `try-catch`.

## Null checks
Use `is null` / `is not null`, not `== null` / `!= null`. Prefer `??` / `??=` over ternary null checks.

```csharp
// BAD
if (obj == null)
{
    return;
}
string value = myString != null ? myString : "default";

// GOOD
if (obj is null)
{
    return;
}
var value = myString ?? "default";
```

## Operators
When splitting `&&` / `||` across lines, put the operator at the end of the line.

```csharp
// BAD
if (user.IsActive
    && user.IsVerified)

// GOOD
if (user.IsActive &&
    user.IsVerified)
```

## Chaining / LINQ
One method call per line for fluent and LINQ chains. Prefer method syntax over query syntax.

```csharp
// BAD
var user = await Db.Users.Where(u => u.Id == userId).Include(u => u.Profile).FirstOrDefaultAsync(ct);

// GOOD
var user = await Db.Users
    .Where(u => u.Id == userId)
    .Include(u => u.Profile)
    .FirstOrDefaultAsync(ct);
```

## `var`
Always use `var` for local variables when the type is obvious from the right-hand side. Prefer `var` over an explicit type whenever the compiler can infer it.

```csharp
// BAD
string name = "Alice";
List<User> users = GetUsers();
User user = new User();

// GOOD
var name = "Alice";
var users = GetUsers();
var user = new User();
```

## Naming
- Types, methods, properties, constants: PascalCase
- Locals and parameters: camelCase

## Async
Prefer `async`/`await`. Do not use `.Result` or `.Wait()`. I/O methods return `Task` / `Task<T>`.
